# 50 — denormal float literals survive ANGLE's compiler. Unity's HLSL→GLSL
# translator keeps integers in float registers and writes such a value as a
# literal: intBitsToFloat(9) appears as 1.26116862e-44. ANGLE's lexer turns any
# literal below FLT_MIN into 0.0 unless preserveDenorms is set, which its Metal
# backend never sets; a loop whose exit value was such a literal (a parallax
# shader's step counter) then restarted forever and the GPU watchdog killed the
# session. In a GLSL ES 3.00+ shader each nonzero literal below FLT_MIN is
# rewritten as intBitsToFloat(<its exact bits>), which compiles to a bit cast
# and keeps the value. The first few rewrites are logged.
python3 - "$K/runtime/gfx/kl_glfb.c" <<'PYEOF'
