# 65 — a destroyed image's storage is released even while views of it live.
# In MoltenVK a view retains its image, so a vkDestroyImage with views still
# alive frees nothing: the image and its full-size texture live on. One engine
# build deletes a render target's image every frame but keeps the views behind
# its own reference count, so each frame left a full-size depth target behind
# and the app reached the memory limit in two seconds. (Emptying the texture
# with setPurgeableState freed nothing: MoltenVK still held it resident.) When
# the guest destroys an image that still has views, a quarter second later the
# image is given a 1x1 texture of the same shape through MoltenVK's own
# vkSetMTLTextureMVK — which releases the old texture and its cached views —
# and each of those views is rebuilt on it. No view handle is touched (an early
# view destroy let handles be reused under the guest — step 51), and a view of a
# destroyed image is invalid in Vulkan, so nothing valid reads the stand-in.
# KL_VK_REBASE_ORPHANS=0 turns it off.
